1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
(text softly clacking)
(suspenseful music)

2
00:00:07,430 --> 00:00:11,076
(suspenseful music drowns out speaker)

3
00:00:11,076 --> 00:00:16,076
(bushes rustling)
(videographer breathing)

4
00:00:17,190 --> 00:00:19,840
- [Gregor] They're just everywhere.

5
00:00:19,840 --> 00:00:22,141
Whoa, this is really blue.

6
00:00:22,141 --> 00:00:24,240
You should come look, this is so blue.

7
00:00:24,240 --> 00:00:26,970
- [Researcher] Look at
how many you're finding.

8
00:00:26,970 --> 00:00:30,170
- [Gregor] I feel
like Gollum, my precious!

9
00:00:30,170 --> 00:00:31,650
if it weren't so dangerous,

10
00:00:31,650 --> 00:00:33,420
it'd be a fun activity to do.

11
00:00:33,420 --> 00:00:35,070
- [Researcher] These are the big chunks,

12
00:00:35,070 --> 00:00:38,190
so what about all the
particles you can't see?

13
00:00:38,190 --> 00:00:39,630
- This same kind of material

14
00:00:39,630 --> 00:00:40,920
was used in the construction

15
00:00:40,920 --> 00:00:42,720
of the World Trade Center buildings.

16
00:00:42,720 --> 00:00:44,190
And when the towers fell,

17
00:00:44,190 --> 00:00:46,170
it was pulverized to microscopic size

18
00:00:46,170 --> 00:00:47,763
and released into the air.

19
00:00:48,960 --> 00:00:50,940
The particles remained airborne for days

20
00:00:50,940 --> 00:00:54,030
and thousands of people
unknowingly breathed them in.

21
00:00:54,030 --> 00:00:56,940
They buried themselves
deep within people's lungs,

22
00:00:56,940 --> 00:00:59,853
wreaking havoc and causing
all sorts of diseases.

23
00:01:01,410 --> 00:01:02,490
We've known for decades

24
00:01:02,490 --> 00:01:04,980
that these particles
are extremely dangerous,

25
00:01:04,980 --> 00:01:07,353
but when the towers
fell, no one was warned.

26
00:01:09,420 --> 00:01:11,340
- The concentrations are such

27
00:01:11,340 --> 00:01:13,563
that they don't pose a health hazard.

28
00:01:14,670 --> 00:01:17,280
- [Gregor] And yet today the
diseases linked to that dust

29
00:01:17,280 --> 00:01:19,380
have killed more than twice as many people

30
00:01:19,380 --> 00:01:20,823
as the attacks themselves.

31
00:01:21,840 --> 00:01:23,073
Once we started looking,

32
00:01:24,750 --> 00:01:28,620
we kept finding this material
in places we never expected.

33
00:01:28,620 --> 00:01:29,917
- He was telling the newspapers,

34
00:01:29,917 --> 00:01:31,110
"People aren't just eating it

35
00:01:31,110 --> 00:01:32,850
and breathing it, they're mainlining it."

36
00:01:32,850 --> 00:01:35,400
- [Gregor] In popular
off-roading spots, in makeup,

37
00:01:35,400 --> 00:01:37,380
and even kids toys.

38
00:01:37,380 --> 00:01:39,630
- [Sean] Say It Ain't
So Mickey Mouse crayons.

39
00:01:39,630 --> 00:01:40,860
- No!

40
00:01:40,860 --> 00:01:42,450
It's been detected in the dust

41
00:01:42,450 --> 00:01:44,070
around schools and homes.

42
00:01:44,070 --> 00:01:46,290
- Five generations of
people died up there.

43
00:01:46,290 --> 00:01:48,270
- [Gregor] And instead
of banning it outright,

44
00:01:48,270 --> 00:01:50,107
we let it spread.

45
00:01:50,107 --> 00:01:52,800
(camera shutter clicking)

46
00:01:52,800 --> 00:01:55,560
Some countries are still
importing hundreds of thousands

47
00:01:55,560 --> 00:01:56,393
of tons each year,

48
00:01:56,393 --> 00:01:58,440
and it's estimated that by 2035,

49
00:01:58,440 --> 00:02:01,867
nearly 2.8 million people
might die because of it.

50
00:02:01,867 --> 00:02:04,617
(dramatic music)

51
00:02:06,360 --> 00:02:08,970
This is a video about a
deadly miracle material

52
00:02:08,970 --> 00:02:10,713
we can't stop using.

53
00:02:11,910 --> 00:02:13,230
This investigation is based

54
00:02:13,230 --> 00:02:15,480
on publicly available
documents, recordings,

55
00:02:15,480 --> 00:02:16,950
and third party sources.

56
00:02:16,950 --> 00:02:18,810
All of our links are in the description.

57
00:02:18,810 --> 00:02:21,240
Thank you to Ground News
for sponsoring this video.

58
00:02:21,240 --> 00:02:22,443
More about them later.

59
00:02:24,870 --> 00:02:26,730
There is this story
about the ancient Greeks

60
00:02:26,730 --> 00:02:29,130
from around the second century AD.

61
00:02:29,130 --> 00:02:30,990
They had this golden lantern

62
00:02:30,990 --> 00:02:34,050
that would burn for a whole
year without going out,

63
00:02:34,050 --> 00:02:35,760
all because of a very special wick

64
00:02:35,760 --> 00:02:37,680
that just wouldn't burn down.

65
00:02:37,680 --> 00:02:40,800
So how did they develop this technology?

66
00:02:40,800 --> 00:02:44,193
Well, the truth is they
didn't, they found it.

67
00:02:46,230 --> 00:02:48,570
Imagine you're walking
around 2,000 years ago

68
00:02:48,570 --> 00:02:51,780
and you see this fluffy looking
stuff poking out the ground.

69
00:02:51,780 --> 00:02:54,630
It's got all of these fibers
that you can pull apart

70
00:02:54,630 --> 00:02:56,193
and twist into shapes.

71
00:03:00,180 --> 00:03:03,150
- [Sean] First off,
let's get a nice bundle.

72
00:03:03,150 --> 00:03:04,740
So it looks like cotton.

73
00:03:04,740 --> 00:03:06,300
- [Gregor] It looks like
it would burn really well,

74
00:03:06,300 --> 00:03:07,650
like it would just-
- Yeah,

75
00:03:07,650 --> 00:03:10,050
you can start a fire
with it, right, alright.

76
00:03:10,050 --> 00:03:11,753
- [Gregor] Okay, let's see what happens.

77
00:03:13,050 --> 00:03:14,610
- [Sean] It's not burning.

78
00:03:14,610 --> 00:03:17,070
- That's because this is actually a rock.

79
00:03:17,070 --> 00:03:18,820
It's a naturally occurring mineral.

80
00:03:19,890 --> 00:03:21,450
The core building block is simple.

81
00:03:21,450 --> 00:03:25,110
It's a silicon atom surrounded
by four oxygen atoms.

82
00:03:25,110 --> 00:03:28,350
Now silicon has four
electrons in its outer shell,

83
00:03:28,350 --> 00:03:29,910
but it really wants eight.

84
00:03:29,910 --> 00:03:33,780
So each of the oxygens
shares one electron with it,

85
00:03:33,780 --> 00:03:35,490
but oxygen doesn't share evenly

86
00:03:35,490 --> 00:03:37,740
since it's more
electronegative than silicon,

87
00:03:37,740 --> 00:03:41,040
it pulls those shared
electrons closer to itself.

88
00:03:41,040 --> 00:03:43,680
This leaves the oxygen
side slightly negative

89
00:03:43,680 --> 00:03:46,410
and the silicon side slightly positive.

90
00:03:46,410 --> 00:03:49,500
Now there's an electrostatic
attraction between these atoms,

91
00:03:49,500 --> 00:03:51,360
which pulls the atoms closer together

92
00:03:51,360 --> 00:03:53,460
and strengthens this bond.

93
00:03:53,460 --> 00:03:56,430
The result is an incredibly
stable pyramid shaped unit

94
00:03:56,430 --> 00:03:58,620
called as silica tetrahedron.

95
00:03:58,620 --> 00:04:01,860
But if you look at the
oxygens in these corner spots,

96
00:04:01,860 --> 00:04:03,690
they've only shared one electron,

97
00:04:03,690 --> 00:04:07,080
but they actually want two to
complete their outer shells.

98
00:04:07,080 --> 00:04:09,870
So the corners link up
with other silicon atoms

99
00:04:09,870 --> 00:04:11,460
to form more tetrahedra.

100
00:04:11,460 --> 00:04:13,960
And in this way, the
structure just keeps growing.

101
00:04:15,330 --> 00:04:18,120
The bonds inside here are
incredibly strong and stable,

102
00:04:18,120 --> 00:04:20,490
and because the atoms
are already tightly bound

103
00:04:20,490 --> 00:04:23,010
to the oxygens inside
the silicate structure,

104
00:04:23,010 --> 00:04:25,680
the oxygen in the air has
nothing to react with,

105
00:04:25,680 --> 00:04:27,183
so the material doesn't burn.

106
00:04:29,280 --> 00:04:30,990
But overall, there's nothing special

107
00:04:30,990 --> 00:04:32,310
about these building blocks.

108
00:04:32,310 --> 00:04:34,200
More than 90% of the Earth's minerals

109
00:04:34,200 --> 00:04:35,580
are made from this stuff,

110
00:04:35,580 --> 00:04:37,533
everything from quartz to clay.

111
00:04:38,580 --> 00:04:40,530
What makes this material special is

112
00:04:40,530 --> 00:04:42,183
how those units link up.

113
00:04:43,140 --> 00:04:45,450
Here, the tetrahedra have formed a sheet,

114
00:04:45,450 --> 00:04:47,850
and bonded to it there is
actually a second sheet

115
00:04:47,850 --> 00:04:49,470
made of magnesium atoms

116
00:04:49,470 --> 00:04:51,990
and hydroxyl groups,
which are just an oxygen

117
00:04:51,990 --> 00:04:53,700
and a hydrogen stuck together.

118
00:04:53,700 --> 00:04:55,170
Now, the atomic spacings

119
00:04:55,170 --> 00:04:57,660
of these two layers
are slightly different.

120
00:04:57,660 --> 00:05:00,480
So there's a tiny mismatch,
which causes tension

121
00:05:00,480 --> 00:05:03,420
between these layers
causing them to curl up,

122
00:05:03,420 --> 00:05:06,030
and you end up with these
tiny scroll-like tubes.

123
00:05:06,030 --> 00:05:08,820
These tubes don't break
down easily under heat.

124
00:05:08,820 --> 00:05:12,630
The structure stays stable up
to around 600 degrees Celsius.

125
00:05:12,630 --> 00:05:14,310
So like all these individual fibers

126
00:05:14,310 --> 00:05:15,390
that you see running through here,

127
00:05:15,390 --> 00:05:16,939
those are all like these curls?

128
00:05:16,939 --> 00:05:17,772
- Yes.

129
00:05:17,772 --> 00:05:18,660
- What is that, what was that there?

130
00:05:18,660 --> 00:05:20,520
- [Sean] That's just a place

131
00:05:20,520 --> 00:05:22,965
where I twisted the
fiber with my tweezers.

132
00:05:22,965 --> 00:05:24,330
- Oh, okay.

133
00:05:24,330 --> 00:05:25,980
And when you twist these fibers,

134
00:05:25,980 --> 00:05:27,360
they actually don't break.

135
00:05:27,360 --> 00:05:29,943
So it's literally a rock you can weave.

136
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:33,900
And when you do,

137
00:05:33,900 --> 00:05:36,450
the fibers form a tangled,
layered structure.

138
00:05:36,450 --> 00:05:39,150
So if heat is introduced,
it has to pass from fiber

139
00:05:39,150 --> 00:05:41,550
to fiber across many contact points

140
00:05:41,550 --> 00:05:43,620
with air filling the spaces between them.

141
00:05:43,620 --> 00:05:46,110
This reduces how quickly heat can spread

142
00:05:46,110 --> 00:05:47,340
through the material.

143
00:05:47,340 --> 00:05:49,770
Because of that, people
started weaving it into things

144
00:05:49,770 --> 00:05:50,880
like theater curtains

145
00:05:50,880 --> 00:05:53,460
and insulation blankets for steam engines,

146
00:05:53,460 --> 00:05:55,020
even fireproof clothing,

147
00:05:55,020 --> 00:05:55,890
essentially anywhere

148
00:05:55,890 --> 00:05:57,990
they didn't want something to catch fire.

149
00:05:57,990 --> 00:05:59,970
But by far the most important use

150
00:05:59,970 --> 00:06:02,370
came around in the 1800s.

151
00:06:02,370 --> 00:06:04,590
Between 1790 and 1870,

152
00:06:04,590 --> 00:06:07,680
the number of people living
in urban areas in America

153
00:06:07,680 --> 00:06:11,280
jumped from 1 in 20 to around 1 in 4.

154
00:06:11,280 --> 00:06:13,710
So to accommodate this,
people had to tack on

155
00:06:13,710 --> 00:06:15,720
extra floors onto existing buildings

156
00:06:15,720 --> 00:06:17,580
and courtyards would then be filled

157
00:06:17,580 --> 00:06:18,930
with makeshift extensions,

158
00:06:18,930 --> 00:06:22,320
effectively tightly packing all
of these buildings together.

159
00:06:22,320 --> 00:06:25,320
Pretty much all these buildings
were made out of wood,

160
00:06:25,320 --> 00:06:28,890
but the people inside still
cooked with open flames.

161
00:06:28,890 --> 00:06:31,290
They used gas lamps, they lit candles.

162
00:06:31,290 --> 00:06:32,280
So one accident

163
00:06:32,280 --> 00:06:35,430
and an entire neighborhood
could go up in flames.

164
00:06:35,430 --> 00:06:39,540
That reality hit New York
City in December, 1835,

165
00:06:39,540 --> 00:06:41,670
when within a span of just two days,

166
00:06:41,670 --> 00:06:44,193
three separate fires erupted in Manhattan.

167
00:06:45,210 --> 00:06:48,960
One bystander described what
followed as "An ocean of fire

168
00:06:48,960 --> 00:06:51,777
with roaring, rolling, burning waves."

169
00:06:53,730 --> 00:06:57,090
By the end, a third of a mile
of Manhattan was engulfed,

170
00:06:57,090 --> 00:07:01,410
destroying nearly 700 buildings
at a cost of $20 million.

171
00:07:01,410 --> 00:07:05,130
That's over $730 million of today's money.

172
00:07:05,130 --> 00:07:07,350
Similar catastrophes
were happening in cities

173
00:07:07,350 --> 00:07:12,090
all over the world, Chicago,
London, Hamburg, Tokyo.

174
00:07:12,090 --> 00:07:13,620
- [Reporter] When will this appalling rate

175
00:07:13,620 --> 00:07:15,273
of destruction come to an end?

176
00:07:18,465 --> 00:07:20,190
- [Gregor] The problem was
that when a building burned,

177
00:07:20,190 --> 00:07:22,980
it spewed up embers into the air.

178
00:07:22,980 --> 00:07:24,540
These then got carried by the wind

179
00:07:24,540 --> 00:07:27,483
and landed onto other
roofs, setting them alight.

180
00:07:30,210 --> 00:07:32,940
So 23 years after the
Great Fire of New York,

181
00:07:32,940 --> 00:07:36,300
a 21-year-old named
Henry Ward Johns set out

182
00:07:36,300 --> 00:07:40,200
to break that chain reaction
by making roofs fireproof.

183
00:07:40,200 --> 00:07:41,850
But that's trickier than it sounds.

184
00:07:41,850 --> 00:07:43,080
Whatever his solution was,

185
00:07:43,080 --> 00:07:45,360
it had to be usable across an entire city.

186
00:07:45,360 --> 00:07:48,210
So cheap and easy enough to mass produce,

187
00:07:48,210 --> 00:07:50,730
durable enough to sit exposed on rooftops

188
00:07:50,730 --> 00:07:53,400
baking in the summer sun,
freezing in the winter,

189
00:07:53,400 --> 00:07:56,100
and most importantly, it
was not allowed to ignite

190
00:07:56,100 --> 00:07:58,593
even when exposed to burning embers.

191
00:07:59,970 --> 00:08:01,710
Now, Johns knew of a mineral

192
00:08:01,710 --> 00:08:04,710
that was already being
spun into fireproof fabric,

193
00:08:04,710 --> 00:08:07,530
but only the long fibers
were useful for thread.

194
00:08:07,530 --> 00:08:10,380
The shorter ones were
actually swept aside as waste.

195
00:08:10,380 --> 00:08:13,890
Johns realized those scraps
were exactly what he needed,

196
00:08:13,890 --> 00:08:17,100
fireproof, tough and
most importantly, cheap.

197
00:08:17,100 --> 00:08:19,800
So he set up a makeshift lab
in his basement apartment

198
00:08:19,800 --> 00:08:21,540
and started experimenting.

199
00:08:21,540 --> 00:08:25,470
He heated up tar in his tea
kettle, smeared that onto cloth,

200
00:08:25,470 --> 00:08:27,780
and then pressed in these tiny fibers.

201
00:08:27,780 --> 00:08:29,190
Then he ringed the whole thing

202
00:08:29,190 --> 00:08:31,710
through his wife's brand
new clothes ringer,

203
00:08:31,710 --> 00:08:35,313
and when he tested it, it
worked, it didn't burn.

204
00:08:36,510 --> 00:08:39,870
In 1868, Henry Ward Johns
patented his invention,

205
00:08:39,870 --> 00:08:41,550
and by 1927,

206
00:08:41,550 --> 00:08:45,000
the company he built was
generating $45 million

207
00:08:45,000 --> 00:08:49,050
in annual sales, more than
800 million in today's money.

208
00:08:49,050 --> 00:08:51,630
Soon people were using
this fire-resistant stuff

209
00:08:51,630 --> 00:08:53,700
in all kinds of building materials,

210
00:08:53,700 --> 00:08:55,170
across America, consumption grew

211
00:08:55,170 --> 00:08:58,530
from around 20,400 tons in 1900

212
00:08:58,530 --> 00:09:03,530
to a peak of 803,000 tons in 1973.

213
00:09:03,585 --> 00:09:05,400
(upbeat music)

214
00:09:05,400 --> 00:09:08,190
Because of that, pretty much
every building in the US,

215
00:09:08,190 --> 00:09:10,530
public or private,
commercial or residential,

216
00:09:10,530 --> 00:09:12,780
used some form of this material.

217
00:09:12,780 --> 00:09:15,030
During that same period,
stronger building codes,

218
00:09:15,030 --> 00:09:16,230
safer heating systems

219
00:09:16,230 --> 00:09:19,410
and other fire-resistant
materials were also introduced,

220
00:09:19,410 --> 00:09:20,460
and it showed,

221
00:09:20,460 --> 00:09:24,420
during that time, fire-related
deaths dropped around 80%.

222
00:09:24,420 --> 00:09:25,253
So this material

223
00:09:25,253 --> 00:09:28,571
likely helped save millions
of lives worldwide.

224
00:09:28,571 --> 00:09:31,154
(lively music)

225
00:09:33,390 --> 00:09:35,460
Because it couldn't be destroyed by fire,

226
00:09:35,460 --> 00:09:39,300
the name the ancient Greeks
gave it, it stuck around.

227
00:09:39,300 --> 00:09:42,640
They called it
inextinguishable,

228
00:09:42,640 --> 00:09:43,893
or asbestos.

229
00:09:45,841 --> 00:09:48,360
(bright music)

230
00:09:48,360 --> 00:09:50,820
- [Presenter] Asbestos,
the remarkable mineral.

231
00:09:50,820 --> 00:09:53,370
- [Gregor] By the mid 20th
century, asbestos was everywhere,

232
00:09:53,370 --> 00:09:56,850
inside brake pads,
toasters, ironing boards,

233
00:09:56,850 --> 00:10:00,330
hair dryers, surgical
dressings, and blankets.

234
00:10:00,330 --> 00:10:03,060
You know, brewers
filtered beer through it.

235
00:10:03,060 --> 00:10:07,530
One brand of toothpaste even
used it for extra polish,

236
00:10:07,530 --> 00:10:09,540
the fake snow in department store windows

237
00:10:09,540 --> 00:10:11,760
and in movies like "The Wizard of Oz,"

238
00:10:11,760 --> 00:10:13,263
all of that's asbestos too.

239
00:10:14,520 --> 00:10:16,770
- Unusual weather we're having, eh?

240
00:10:16,770 --> 00:10:18,630
- [Gregor] Sorry, Dorothy.

241
00:10:18,630 --> 00:10:19,800
- It was such a big deal,

242
00:10:19,800 --> 00:10:23,490
Marvel even had a villain
called Asbestos Lady.

243
00:10:23,490 --> 00:10:25,620
She'd set a fire to escape the police,

244
00:10:25,620 --> 00:10:26,790
and she'd easily walk through it,

245
00:10:26,790 --> 00:10:28,743
safe inside her asbestos bodysuit.

246
00:10:30,960 --> 00:10:32,130
To feed this demand,

247
00:10:32,130 --> 00:10:35,310
asbestos was pulled out of the
ground on an enormous scale.

248
00:10:35,310 --> 00:10:38,310
Major mining operations
spread across Canada, Russia,

249
00:10:38,310 --> 00:10:39,540
and South Africa

250
00:10:39,540 --> 00:10:41,220
with global production peaking

251
00:10:41,220 --> 00:10:46,053
at approximately 4.8 million
tons per year in 1977.

252
00:10:49,080 --> 00:10:50,820
But the reason asbestos ended up

253
00:10:50,820 --> 00:10:53,160
in so many different products is

254
00:10:53,160 --> 00:10:56,313
because it's actually a
group of different minerals,

255
00:10:57,510 --> 00:11:00,060
that white, fluffy stuff
we tried to burn earlier,

256
00:11:00,060 --> 00:11:01,470
it's called chrysotile,

257
00:11:01,470 --> 00:11:04,890
and it belongs to a mineral
family known as the serpentines.

258
00:11:04,890 --> 00:11:07,690
But other types of asbestos
looked completely different.

259
00:11:08,610 --> 00:11:12,840
For instance, there is also
brown asbestos known as amosite.

260
00:11:12,840 --> 00:11:16,530
It forms thick fibers that
almost look like wood splinters,

261
00:11:16,530 --> 00:11:19,770
strong, stable, and highly heat resistant.

262
00:11:19,770 --> 00:11:22,620
So it was perfect for putting
into building materials

263
00:11:22,620 --> 00:11:23,943
like cement panels.

264
00:11:24,990 --> 00:11:27,780
This type belongs to a
different mineral family,

265
00:11:27,780 --> 00:11:32,010
the amphiboles, here,
instead of forming sheets,

266
00:11:32,010 --> 00:11:35,940
the silica tetrahedra lock
into rigid, ladder-like chains,

267
00:11:35,940 --> 00:11:38,160
and amosite, iron and magnesium ions,

268
00:11:38,160 --> 00:11:40,920
along with hydroxyl groups
embedded in the structure,

269
00:11:40,920 --> 00:11:42,510
bind those chains together,

270
00:11:42,510 --> 00:11:45,213
forming these long, needle-like fibers,

271
00:11:46,800 --> 00:11:49,620
but tweak that chemistry just
slightly so that now iron

272
00:11:49,620 --> 00:11:51,690
and sodium ions bind the chains,

273
00:11:51,690 --> 00:11:54,723
and you get this, blue
asbestos or crocidolite.

274
00:11:56,190 --> 00:11:58,650
These crystals split
easily along their length

275
00:11:58,650 --> 00:12:01,320
and they create these fine flexible fibers

276
00:12:01,320 --> 00:12:03,150
that are still extraordinarily strong

277
00:12:03,150 --> 00:12:06,723
with tensile strengths comparable
to high grade steel wire.

278
00:12:08,280 --> 00:12:10,950
This type went into
chemical-resistant insulation,

279
00:12:10,950 --> 00:12:14,700
shipyards, and even filters
inside early gas masks.

280
00:12:14,700 --> 00:12:15,660
Oh, and there was another use,

281
00:12:15,660 --> 00:12:18,210
one that's hard to believe now.

282
00:12:18,210 --> 00:12:21,150
- In this magic box I have right here

283
00:12:21,150 --> 00:12:24,360
is something that was manufactured

284
00:12:24,360 --> 00:12:25,893
right here in North Carolina.

285
00:12:26,880 --> 00:12:30,930
They're cigarettes, produced in the 1950s,

286
00:12:30,930 --> 00:12:33,360
and if you look at the filter,

287
00:12:33,360 --> 00:12:38,999
you see the filters are blue, asbestos.

288
00:12:39,900 --> 00:12:43,500
This is Kent with a Micronite
filter that was manufactured

289
00:12:43,500 --> 00:12:46,387
with crocidolite asbestos
in the filter itself.

290
00:12:46,387 --> 00:12:48,750
- So you're not only
smoking, you were smoking it

291
00:12:48,750 --> 00:12:50,370
through a blue asbestos filter.

292
00:12:50,370 --> 00:12:52,290
Yes, what a deal.

293
00:12:52,290 --> 00:12:55,860
- Only Kent has the revolutionary
new Micronite filter

294
00:12:55,860 --> 00:12:57,120
you've heard so much about.

295
00:12:57,120 --> 00:13:02,120
Kent and only Kent filters best,
filters best, filters best.

296
00:13:02,129 --> 00:13:07,129
(tobacco crackling)
(suspenseful music)

297
00:13:10,961 --> 00:13:12,390
- [Gregor] In the early 1900s,

298
00:13:12,390 --> 00:13:16,230
a young woman named Nelly
Kershaw worked in a factory

299
00:13:16,230 --> 00:13:19,110
that spun asbestos fibers into threads.

300
00:13:19,110 --> 00:13:21,210
Every day she breathed in the dust

301
00:13:21,210 --> 00:13:23,880
that those machines threw into the air.

302
00:13:23,880 --> 00:13:25,830
So by her early thirties,

303
00:13:25,830 --> 00:13:28,623
she was so sick she could barely breathe.

304
00:13:29,940 --> 00:13:33,300
And when she finally decided
to ask the factory for help,

305
00:13:33,300 --> 00:13:35,820
they refused, they said
helping out workers

306
00:13:35,820 --> 00:13:38,370
would set a dangerous precedent.

307
00:13:38,370 --> 00:13:41,883
Nelly died shortly after
at the age of just 33.

308
00:13:43,440 --> 00:13:44,970
Nelly's case caught the attention

309
00:13:44,970 --> 00:13:47,640
of pathologist Dr. William Cook.

310
00:13:47,640 --> 00:13:50,520
When he opened up her
chest, her lungs were gray

311
00:13:50,520 --> 00:13:52,260
and scarred, almost blue-black,

312
00:13:52,260 --> 00:13:54,600
like they had a huge internal bruise.

313
00:13:54,600 --> 00:13:58,500
And when his scalpel passed
through them, they rasped.

314
00:13:58,500 --> 00:14:00,693
It was like scraping against sandpaper.

315
00:14:02,190 --> 00:14:03,990
The tissue was full of mineral grit,

316
00:14:03,990 --> 00:14:06,960
and under the microscope
the cause was unmistakable,

317
00:14:06,960 --> 00:14:09,483
asbestos fibers lodged
into the lung tissue.

318
00:14:11,430 --> 00:14:14,250
- If we were to inhale some
type of an asbestos fiber,

319
00:14:14,250 --> 00:14:15,390
I kind of won't think of them

320
00:14:15,390 --> 00:14:17,640
as like little microscopic
straight arrows,

321
00:14:17,640 --> 00:14:20,940
they kind of just shoot down
through the nose or the mouth

322
00:14:20,940 --> 00:14:23,460
and move down through the trachea.

323
00:14:23,460 --> 00:14:26,340
If we continue on going
down here, we get smaller

324
00:14:26,340 --> 00:14:29,010
and smaller as we penetrate
deeper into the lung tissue.

325
00:14:29,010 --> 00:14:31,380
And then you get into these alveolar sacs,

326
00:14:31,380 --> 00:14:34,020
these asbestos fibers, they
lodge in the tissue there,

327
00:14:34,020 --> 00:14:37,710
and lung secretions, enzymes,
even white blood cells,

328
00:14:37,710 --> 00:14:40,500
they have a really hard
time breaking those down.

329
00:14:40,500 --> 00:14:42,950
- You end up with scarring
deep inside the lungs.

330
00:14:44,417 --> 00:14:47,670
In 1924, Dr. Cook published
the first medical description

331
00:14:47,670 --> 00:14:51,003
of this condition, which
became known as asbestosis.

332
00:14:52,140 --> 00:14:55,050
When these asbestos fibers
lodge into the lungs,

333
00:14:55,050 --> 00:14:57,090
the body treats them like invaders,

334
00:14:57,090 --> 00:15:00,120
specialized cells called
macrophages move in,

335
00:15:00,120 --> 00:15:01,980
cells whose job it is to engulf

336
00:15:01,980 --> 00:15:04,830
and digest bacteria, dust or debris.

337
00:15:04,830 --> 00:15:08,460
But asbestos fibers are too
long and stiff to swallow.

338
00:15:08,460 --> 00:15:11,250
It's kind of like trying to
eat a toothpick sideways.

339
00:15:11,250 --> 00:15:13,980
The macrophages keep trying and failing,

340
00:15:13,980 --> 00:15:16,770
and in the process, they
release inflammatory chemicals

341
00:15:16,770 --> 00:15:19,140
that damage the surrounding lung tissue.

342
00:15:19,140 --> 00:15:22,080
So workers breathing in
asbestos dust day after day,

343
00:15:22,080 --> 00:15:24,990
accumulated more and more damage.

344
00:15:24,990 --> 00:15:26,010
When doctors sent

345
00:15:26,010 --> 00:15:28,260
by the British government
examined hundreds

346
00:15:28,260 --> 00:15:29,670
of asbestos workers,

347
00:15:29,670 --> 00:15:31,920
they found that more than 25%

348
00:15:31,920 --> 00:15:34,650
already showed signs of lung disease.

349
00:15:34,650 --> 00:15:37,470
And for workers with over
20 years of exposure,

350
00:15:37,470 --> 00:15:40,050
that number was closer to 80%.

351
00:15:40,050 --> 00:15:43,860
So in 1931, the government
officially classified asbestos

352
00:15:43,860 --> 00:15:45,480
as a workplace hazard,

353
00:15:45,480 --> 00:15:47,520
making it one of the
first industrial materials

354
00:15:47,520 --> 00:15:49,650
to be regulated for health risks.

355
00:15:49,650 --> 00:15:51,780
But the new rules only covered factories

356
00:15:51,780 --> 00:15:53,940
where asbestos was manufactured.

357
00:15:53,940 --> 00:15:57,120
They didn't extend to other
workers like ship builders,

358
00:15:57,120 --> 00:16:00,690
miners or construction workers
who were regularly exposed

359
00:16:00,690 --> 00:16:02,043
to asbestos dust.

360
00:16:04,230 --> 00:16:06,450
Across the Atlantic,
things weren't much better.

361
00:16:06,450 --> 00:16:10,050
There was no binding federal
asbestos rules in the States,

362
00:16:10,050 --> 00:16:11,730
only a recommendation.

363
00:16:11,730 --> 00:16:13,170
The US Public Health Service

364
00:16:13,170 --> 00:16:15,210
suggested a temporary exposure limit

365
00:16:15,210 --> 00:16:17,100
of 5 million asbestos particles

366
00:16:17,100 --> 00:16:19,320
for a single cubic foot of air,

367
00:16:19,320 --> 00:16:21,240
which meant that a
worker breathing normally

368
00:16:21,240 --> 00:16:25,110
could inhale over 300 million
asbestos particles an hour

369
00:16:25,110 --> 00:16:27,690
and still be considered within guidelines.

370
00:16:27,690 --> 00:16:30,390
This became especially
problematic for shipyard workers

371
00:16:30,390 --> 00:16:31,950
when World War II broke out,

372
00:16:31,950 --> 00:16:34,860
ships were packed with
asbestos insulation.

373
00:16:34,860 --> 00:16:36,900
So workers spent their days cutting

374
00:16:36,900 --> 00:16:40,170
and fitting asbestos in
thick clouds of fibers.

375
00:16:40,170 --> 00:16:42,030
And according to the
guidelines of the day,

376
00:16:42,030 --> 00:16:44,400
these levels met the official definition

377
00:16:44,400 --> 00:16:46,530
of safe working conditions.

378
00:16:46,530 --> 00:16:50,190
In fact, asbestos was still
marketed as a magic material.

379
00:16:50,190 --> 00:16:51,247
A few years earlier,

380
00:16:51,247 --> 00:16:54,180
"Time" magazine actually put
Johns-Manville's president,

381
00:16:54,180 --> 00:16:57,630
Lewis H. Brown on its
April 3rd, 1939 cover.

382
00:16:57,630 --> 00:16:59,580
But in the early 1960s,

383
00:16:59,580 --> 00:17:03,543
finally one doctor started
connecting the dots on asbestos.

384
00:17:04,770 --> 00:17:07,080
Dr. Irving Selikoff was
running a small clinic

385
00:17:07,080 --> 00:17:08,670
in Patterson, New Jersey,

386
00:17:08,670 --> 00:17:11,250
when the local asbestos
workers Union asked if

387
00:17:11,250 --> 00:17:13,650
their members could come and see him.

388
00:17:13,650 --> 00:17:15,930
Before long, he'd seen multiple workers

389
00:17:15,930 --> 00:17:18,150
with either severe lung scarring

390
00:17:18,150 --> 00:17:19,350
or more concerningly,

391
00:17:19,350 --> 00:17:22,860
an extremely rare cancer
called mesothelioma.

392
00:17:22,860 --> 00:17:26,220
- A mesothelioma is strongly associated

393
00:17:26,220 --> 00:17:27,750
with asbestos exposure

394
00:17:27,750 --> 00:17:31,530
and is cancer of those
cells lining the inside

395
00:17:31,530 --> 00:17:32,520
of the chest cavity.

396
00:17:32,520 --> 00:17:34,890
And most commonly, it's
this pleural cavity.

397
00:17:34,890 --> 00:17:39,000
These pleural membranes are
aligned with mesothelial cells.

398
00:17:39,000 --> 00:17:43,020
Sometimes what happens is the
fibers will work their way out

399
00:17:43,020 --> 00:17:46,767
of the lung tissue and directly
get into this cavity here,

400
00:17:46,767 --> 00:17:49,413
and they can literally
pierce out the lungs.

401
00:17:50,610 --> 00:17:52,410
- They cause constant irritation.

402
00:17:52,410 --> 00:17:55,290
And over time, that can
trigger cancerous changes

403
00:17:55,290 --> 00:17:57,303
in the cells that make up those linings.

404
00:18:00,330 --> 00:18:02,640
Selikoff needed more data
to understand the scale

405
00:18:02,640 --> 00:18:05,220
of the problem, but factory owners refused

406
00:18:05,220 --> 00:18:08,430
to share medical records
from their workers with him.

407
00:18:08,430 --> 00:18:11,070
So Selikoff had to get creative.

408
00:18:11,070 --> 00:18:14,460
See, during World War II,
many shipyard workers employed

409
00:18:14,460 --> 00:18:17,610
by the Navy underwent
federal background checks.

410
00:18:17,610 --> 00:18:20,280
Thousands of these men had
been working with asbestos

411
00:18:20,280 --> 00:18:21,780
to insulate ships.

412
00:18:21,780 --> 00:18:25,680
So using surviving FBI
wartime personnel records,

413
00:18:25,680 --> 00:18:28,230
Selikoff began tracking them down

414
00:18:28,230 --> 00:18:31,260
and one by one painstakingly
pieced together

415
00:18:31,260 --> 00:18:33,000
their medical histories.

416
00:18:33,000 --> 00:18:37,110
What emerged wasn't a handful
of isolated tragedies,

417
00:18:37,110 --> 00:18:38,970
it was a pattern,

418
00:18:38,970 --> 00:18:42,393
that exposure proved
deadlier than combat itself.

419
00:18:43,230 --> 00:18:47,610
8.6 out of every 1,000
servicemen were killed in action,

420
00:18:47,610 --> 00:18:50,910
whereas 14 out of every
1,000 shipyard workers

421
00:18:50,910 --> 00:18:53,643
later died from asbestos-related cancers.

422
00:18:55,410 --> 00:18:57,420
Selikoff launched a formal investigation

423
00:18:57,420 --> 00:19:00,150
into hundreds of asbestos
insulation workers,

424
00:19:00,150 --> 00:19:02,700
and what he found confirmed his fears,

425
00:19:02,700 --> 00:19:05,340
widespread disabling asbestosis,

426
00:19:05,340 --> 00:19:07,410
dozens of cases of mesothelioma,

427
00:19:07,410 --> 00:19:10,080
lung cancer rates roughly
seven times higher

428
00:19:10,080 --> 00:19:10,913
than expected

429
00:19:10,913 --> 00:19:14,100
and a threefold increase in
gastrointestinal cancers.

430
00:19:14,100 --> 00:19:17,460
In 1964, he organized a
conference at the New York Academy

431
00:19:17,460 --> 00:19:19,800
of Sciences where for the first time,

432
00:19:19,800 --> 00:19:22,770
all this evidence was presented
publicly in one place,

433
00:19:22,770 --> 00:19:25,710
and on the record, it marked
the moment when asbestos

434
00:19:25,710 --> 00:19:28,500
stopped being seen as a
modern miracle material

435
00:19:28,500 --> 00:19:30,450
and instead started being recognized

436
00:19:30,450 --> 00:19:32,193
as a public health crisis.

437
00:19:35,820 --> 00:19:37,680
But the asbestos industry fought back

438
00:19:37,680 --> 00:19:39,450
trying to discredit Selikoff.

439
00:19:39,450 --> 00:19:41,280
Industry-funded research groups came out

440
00:19:41,280 --> 00:19:43,590
with papers minimizing
the risk of exposure

441
00:19:43,590 --> 00:19:46,800
and framing Selikoff's
findings as overblown.

442
00:19:46,800 --> 00:19:50,250
They started a coordinated
PR effort to discredit him,

443
00:19:50,250 --> 00:19:52,230
trying to call him alarmist,

444
00:19:52,230 --> 00:19:55,020
and starting a rumor that
he wasn't even a real doctor

445
00:19:55,020 --> 00:19:57,770
just because he got his
medical degree out in Scotland.

446
00:19:58,650 --> 00:20:00,870
But Selikoff kept going.

447
00:20:00,870 --> 00:20:03,840
He kept publishing data on
the devastating health effects

448
00:20:03,840 --> 00:20:05,190
of asbestos exposure,

449
00:20:05,190 --> 00:20:09,030
he worked 18 hour days
documenting every patient

450
00:20:09,030 --> 00:20:09,930
who wrote to him.

451
00:20:09,930 --> 00:20:13,110
He contacted policymakers,
even world leaders,

452
00:20:13,110 --> 00:20:15,873
urging them to take
action against asbestos.

453
00:20:17,340 --> 00:20:21,300
- Now Selikoff, the legendary doctor

454
00:20:21,300 --> 00:20:24,690
who organized this conference in the 1970s

455
00:20:24,690 --> 00:20:27,900
found that intravenous drugs
were being contaminated

456
00:20:27,900 --> 00:20:29,580
by asbestos filtration.

457
00:20:29,580 --> 00:20:30,660
People aren't just eating it

458
00:20:30,660 --> 00:20:32,003
and breathing it, they're mainlining it.

459
00:20:34,410 --> 00:20:37,260
- By the 1970s, no one
could deny it any longer,

460
00:20:37,260 --> 00:20:40,380
miners, factory workers,
shipyard insulators,

461
00:20:40,380 --> 00:20:42,570
people who'd been exposed decades earlier

462
00:20:42,570 --> 00:20:45,030
during the asbestos
boom, were now turning up

463
00:20:45,030 --> 00:20:48,000
with multiple cancers in huge numbers.

464
00:20:48,000 --> 00:20:50,010
- Asbestos exposure is linked

465
00:20:50,010 --> 00:20:51,720
to all sorts of different cancers.

466
00:20:51,720 --> 00:20:54,233
The lung tissue has
lymphatic vessels in it,

467
00:20:54,233 --> 00:20:56,520
but you have 'em
throughout your whole body.

468
00:20:56,520 --> 00:20:58,890
The asbestos fibers sometimes on their own

469
00:20:58,890 --> 00:21:01,950
can migrate into the lymphatic vessels.

470
00:21:01,950 --> 00:21:04,380
Sometimes the white
blood cells will take it

471
00:21:04,380 --> 00:21:05,970
into the lymphatic system.

472
00:21:05,970 --> 00:21:09,060
Once you hit the lymphatic
system, you have the potential

473
00:21:09,060 --> 00:21:11,013
to go anywhere in the human body.

474
00:21:12,000 --> 00:21:13,500
- [Gregor] Autopsies have found fibers

475
00:21:13,500 --> 00:21:15,810
in nearly every organ in the body,

476
00:21:15,810 --> 00:21:19,380
the brain, bone marrow,
spleen, intestines, pancreas,

477
00:21:19,380 --> 00:21:22,410
prostate, ovaries, thyroid, and liver.

478
00:21:22,410 --> 00:21:25,200
And in every tissue those fibers reach,

479
00:21:25,200 --> 00:21:27,570
they set off the same chain reaction.

480
00:21:27,570 --> 00:21:30,330
- I'm imagining these white
blood cells with personalities,

481
00:21:30,330 --> 00:21:31,740
and they get all mad and frustrated

482
00:21:31,740 --> 00:21:34,380
because they can't engulf
this asbestos fiber.

483
00:21:34,380 --> 00:21:36,600
They've coined this
term called essentially,

484
00:21:36,600 --> 00:21:38,610
frustrated phagocytosis.

485
00:21:38,610 --> 00:21:40,290
They start releasing these things

486
00:21:40,290 --> 00:21:42,180
like reactive oxygen species.

487
00:21:42,180 --> 00:21:44,820
They can cause damage
to surrounding cells,

488
00:21:44,820 --> 00:21:48,720
and really important is damage to DNA.

489
00:21:48,720 --> 00:21:50,940
Those cells can start
dividing out of control

490
00:21:50,940 --> 00:21:52,050
when they start to clump together,

491
00:21:52,050 --> 00:21:54,813
and we start to call those
clumps of cells cancer.

492
00:21:55,770 --> 00:21:57,630
- US courts were flooded with lawsuits

493
00:21:57,630 --> 00:21:59,400
against companies like Johns-Manville.

494
00:21:59,400 --> 00:22:02,160
The harm asbestos caused
was well documented.

495
00:22:02,160 --> 00:22:03,660
The information was out there.

496
00:22:03,660 --> 00:22:06,720
Companies should have known
their products were dangerous.

497
00:22:06,720 --> 00:22:09,420
- They should know what's
reasonably available

498
00:22:09,420 --> 00:22:12,900
in the public domain about
the dangers of asbestos.

499
00:22:12,900 --> 00:22:15,330
If they can read asbestos patents,

500
00:22:15,330 --> 00:22:18,180
they can read asbestos pathology papers.

501
00:22:18,180 --> 00:22:19,830
- But the companies denied it.

502
00:22:19,830 --> 00:22:21,900
What was needed was definitive evidence

503
00:22:21,900 --> 00:22:23,790
that the companies knew their products

504
00:22:23,790 --> 00:22:25,140
were killing their workers.

505
00:22:27,510 --> 00:22:29,520
Then an attorney, Carl Ash,

506
00:22:29,520 --> 00:22:32,130
noticed something strange
in the 1974 report

507
00:22:32,130 --> 00:22:35,670
by this huge asbestos company
called Raybestos Manhattan.

508
00:22:35,670 --> 00:22:37,950
See, in this report, the company suggested

509
00:22:37,950 --> 00:22:40,380
that they had actually been
investigating health hazards

510
00:22:40,380 --> 00:22:42,990
of asbestos since the 1930s.

511
00:22:42,990 --> 00:22:44,850
So Ash started digging.

512
00:22:44,850 --> 00:22:47,460
He filed a request for internal documents,

513
00:22:47,460 --> 00:22:51,210
and at first, the company
claimed it couldn't find much.

514
00:22:51,210 --> 00:22:52,770
Then unexpectedly,

515
00:22:52,770 --> 00:22:56,520
Ash was handed a banker's box
stuffed full of documents,

516
00:22:56,520 --> 00:22:58,920
meticulously kept by
Raybestos Manhattan's,

517
00:22:58,920 --> 00:23:01,233
former president, Sumner Simpson.

518
00:23:03,180 --> 00:23:06,990
Back in 1935, a journal contacted Simpson

519
00:23:06,990 --> 00:23:09,750
because they wanted to write
an article about asbestosis.

520
00:23:09,750 --> 00:23:12,270
Shortly after, Simpson himself reached out

521
00:23:12,270 --> 00:23:15,727
to Johns-Manville's lawyer,
Vandiver Brown, saying,

522
00:23:15,727 --> 00:23:17,610
"I think the less said about asbestos,

523
00:23:17,610 --> 00:23:18,690
the better off we are."

524
00:23:18,690 --> 00:23:21,030
To which Brown replied,
"I quite agree with you

525
00:23:21,030 --> 00:23:22,680
that our interests are best served

526
00:23:22,680 --> 00:23:26,520
by having asbestosis receive
the minimum of publicity."

527
00:23:26,520 --> 00:23:29,940
The same papers also revealed
that in the 1930s, Raybestos

528
00:23:29,940 --> 00:23:32,880
and Johns-Manville hired
an external company,

529
00:23:32,880 --> 00:23:36,210
Saranac Laboratories, to do
studies of asbestos on animals.

530
00:23:36,210 --> 00:23:38,550
But the companies insisted on controlling

531
00:23:38,550 --> 00:23:41,070
what from those studies
will be made public.

532
00:23:41,070 --> 00:23:43,027
As a letter from Vandiver points out,

533
00:23:43,027 --> 00:23:44,430
"It is our further understanding

534
00:23:44,430 --> 00:23:47,160
that the results will be
considered the property of those

535
00:23:47,160 --> 00:23:49,110
who are advancing the required funds,

536
00:23:49,110 --> 00:23:51,240
who will determine whether, to what extent

537
00:23:51,240 --> 00:23:53,820
and in what manner they
shall be made public."

538
00:23:53,820 --> 00:23:56,850
A clause to which Saranac
Laboratories said, "Yes."

539
00:23:56,850 --> 00:23:58,590
But after their lead researcher

540
00:23:58,590 --> 00:24:01,830
who was compiling all this
evidence died in 1946,

541
00:24:01,830 --> 00:24:04,290
the companies agreed that
nothing should be published

542
00:24:04,290 --> 00:24:06,600
that contained any objectionable material.

543
00:24:06,600 --> 00:24:09,540
Objectionable meaning
any sort of indication

544
00:24:09,540 --> 00:24:11,130
that asbestos causes cancer.

545
00:24:11,130 --> 00:24:13,230
So when Saranac Laboratories
finished their research,

546
00:24:13,230 --> 00:24:15,780
the companies took the report, edited it,

547
00:24:15,780 --> 00:24:17,190
and just buried the evidence.

548
00:24:17,190 --> 00:24:19,710
Here's an original copy
of that manuscript,

549
00:24:19,710 --> 00:24:23,070
and you can find whole
sections just crossed out.

550
00:24:23,070 --> 00:24:24,960
Other documents were even more damaging.

551
00:24:24,960 --> 00:24:27,840
A Johns-Manville medical
official later testified

552
00:24:27,840 --> 00:24:30,960
that up until 1971, the
company had a policy

553
00:24:30,960 --> 00:24:34,290
of not telling their workers
if their physicals showed signs

554
00:24:34,290 --> 00:24:37,920
of asbestosis or
asbestos-related lung cancers.

555
00:24:37,920 --> 00:24:39,090
And in sworn testimony,

556
00:24:39,090 --> 00:24:42,660
a witness recalled a meeting
they had in the early 1940s

557
00:24:42,660 --> 00:24:44,790
with the president of Johns-Manville,

558
00:24:44,790 --> 00:24:48,360
asking why they weren't
warning workers about asbestos.

559
00:24:48,360 --> 00:24:50,377
As the witness recalls it, they asked,

560
00:24:50,377 --> 00:24:52,500
"Do you mean to tell me
you would let them work

561
00:24:52,500 --> 00:24:54,090
until they dropped dead?"

562
00:24:54,090 --> 00:24:55,807
To which the president replied,

563
00:24:55,807 --> 00:24:58,017
"Yes, we save a lot of money that way."

564
00:25:00,540 --> 00:25:02,130
Once the Sumner Simpson papers got out,

565
00:25:02,130 --> 00:25:04,650
they unlocked a new industrial Watergate.

566
00:25:04,650 --> 00:25:07,650
The industry's standard, "Oh,
