On its own, it's harmless.

772
00:33:52,350 --> 00:33:54,420
The problem was, Libby's vermiculite

773
00:33:54,420 --> 00:33:58,450
formed mixed in with
affable asbestos fibers.

774
00:33:58,450 --> 00:34:01,320
And the same thing happens
with other minerals we mine,

775
00:34:01,320 --> 00:34:03,870
including stuff like talc.

776
00:34:03,870 --> 00:34:05,790
That's how asbestos ends up in products

777
00:34:05,790 --> 00:34:07,653
like the ones we saw at Sean's lab.

778
00:34:09,090 --> 00:34:11,370
And the worst part, the company that owned

779
00:34:11,370 --> 00:34:14,910
and operated the mine,
W.R. Grace, they knew,

780
00:34:14,910 --> 00:34:16,830
they knew the ore contained asbestos.

781
00:34:16,830 --> 00:34:18,420
They knew people were getting sick

782
00:34:18,420 --> 00:34:20,190
and they didn't warn the town.

783
00:34:20,190 --> 00:34:25,020
In fact, they tried to cover
it up for almost 30 years.

784
00:34:25,020 --> 00:34:27,270
- They had hundreds of workers in there.

785
00:34:27,270 --> 00:34:29,520
And of course, when the
miners would go home,

786
00:34:29,520 --> 00:34:31,290
they had dust all over their clothes,

787
00:34:31,290 --> 00:34:34,620
and their kids and their
wives got it and died as well.

788
00:34:34,620 --> 00:34:36,360
But the doctors up around Libby,

789
00:34:36,360 --> 00:34:38,910
they knew, boy did they know.

790
00:34:38,910 --> 00:34:40,050
- Besides the lung disease

791
00:34:40,050 --> 00:34:43,080
and cancer's long associated
with asbestos exposure,

792
00:34:43,080 --> 00:34:45,000
researchers were also finding rates

793
00:34:45,000 --> 00:34:48,060
of some autoimmune diseases
were nearly six times higher

794
00:34:48,060 --> 00:34:49,380
than the national average.

795
00:34:49,380 --> 00:34:51,240
And by the time the Libby situation

796
00:34:51,240 --> 00:34:53,340
hit the headlines in 1999,

797
00:34:53,340 --> 00:34:56,310
reporters documented nearly 200 deaths

798
00:34:56,310 --> 00:34:58,467
in a town of fewer than 3,000.

799
00:34:58,467 --> 00:35:00,420
- And it could take 20 years for it to go,

800
00:35:00,420 --> 00:35:03,210
but pretty soon you have no breath at all

801
00:35:03,210 --> 00:35:05,100
and you die of asphyxiation.

802
00:35:05,100 --> 00:35:08,670
I could tell I'm talking on
the phone by somebody's voice,

803
00:35:08,670 --> 00:35:10,593
how far along they were toward death,

804
00:35:11,490 --> 00:35:13,230
because none of 'em survived.

805
00:35:13,230 --> 00:35:14,640
- Finally, in 2009,

806
00:35:14,640 --> 00:35:17,970
the EPA declared a public
health emergency in Libby,

807
00:35:17,970 --> 00:35:20,280
calling it "The worst case
of industrial poisoning

808
00:35:20,280 --> 00:35:23,010
of a community in US history."

809
00:35:23,010 --> 00:35:25,293
- But Libby's just the tip of the iceberg,

810
00:35:26,550 --> 00:35:29,850
- Because for decades, W.R.
Grace shipped Libby vermiculite

811
00:35:29,850 --> 00:35:31,020
around the country,

812
00:35:31,020 --> 00:35:33,990
and with it, deadly affable asbestos,

813
00:35:33,990 --> 00:35:37,413
which ended up in millions
of homes as attic insulation.

814
00:35:38,310 --> 00:35:40,350
And Grace also made a fireproof spray

815
00:35:40,350 --> 00:35:43,890
that was used on the steel
frames of high-rise buildings.

816
00:35:43,890 --> 00:35:46,860
By 1970, over half of
the multi-story buildings

817
00:35:46,860 --> 00:35:50,280
erected in the United States
used this fireproof spray,

818
00:35:50,280 --> 00:35:52,263
including the World Trade Center.

819
00:35:53,400 --> 00:35:57,450
But this spray was actually
marketed as asbestos-free.

820
00:35:57,450 --> 00:36:00,600
According to a later investigation
by the "New York Times,"

821
00:36:00,600 --> 00:36:02,310
Grace lobbied regulators

822
00:36:02,310 --> 00:36:05,370
to adopt a threshold under
which products containing less

823
00:36:05,370 --> 00:36:08,610
than 1% of asbestos
would not be regulated.

824
00:36:08,610 --> 00:36:10,110
Grace argued that the danger

825
00:36:10,110 --> 00:36:12,690
of such small amounts had not been proved.

826
00:36:12,690 --> 00:36:16,500
This became known as the
1% rule or the Grace Rule.

827
00:36:16,500 --> 00:36:19,020
That decision didn't
just affect the products

828
00:36:19,020 --> 00:36:20,280
from Libby's mine,

829
00:36:20,280 --> 00:36:23,970
it reshaped how asbestos
was detected, regulated,

830
00:36:23,970 --> 00:36:26,447
and ignored everywhere.

831
00:36:26,447 --> 00:36:30,438
(building roaring)
(sirens wailing)

832
00:36:30,438 --> 00:36:32,532
- [Speaker] Oh my God!
(people chattering)

833
00:36:32,532 --> 00:36:33,721
- [Speaker] Look at the shadow of death!

834
00:36:33,721 --> 00:36:35,430
- [Speaker] Oh my God!

835
00:36:35,430 --> 00:36:37,590
- Okay, when that went down, I knew it.

836
00:36:37,590 --> 00:36:40,200
I knew they had asbestos,
and so I started calling.

837
00:36:40,200 --> 00:36:41,850
I said, "What do you,

838
00:36:41,850 --> 00:36:43,950
how are you gonna protect people from that

839
00:36:43,950 --> 00:36:45,540
'cause now that stuff's
all over the place.

840
00:36:45,540 --> 00:36:47,520
You saw the dust clouds, right?"

841
00:36:47,520 --> 00:36:50,850
- [Gregor] September 11th became
the largest real world test

842
00:36:50,850 --> 00:36:54,690
of asbestos detection following
a single catastrophic event.

843
00:36:54,690 --> 00:36:56,790
- The dust is so thick you can't see.

844
00:36:56,790 --> 00:36:58,740
- When the EPA began sampling the dust

845
00:36:58,740 --> 00:37:01,740
and analyzing it, they chose a
method we use back at the lab

846
00:37:01,740 --> 00:37:04,583
called polarized light microscopy or PLM.

847
00:37:04,583 --> 00:37:07,470
But the PLM has two major limitations.

848
00:37:07,470 --> 00:37:09,600
First, it's struggles to detect asbestos

849
00:37:09,600 --> 00:37:12,930
if it's less than 1% by
weight in the sample,

850
00:37:12,930 --> 00:37:15,420
and second, it can only see the fibers

851
00:37:15,420 --> 00:37:18,330
that are roughly longer
than about five micrometers

852
00:37:18,330 --> 00:37:21,690
or wider than about a
quarter of a micrometer.

853
00:37:21,690 --> 00:37:23,310
As a result, the smallest

854
00:37:23,310 --> 00:37:25,440
and oftentimes the most dangerous fibers,

855
00:37:25,440 --> 00:37:28,380
like the ones pulverized during
the collapse of the towers,

856
00:37:28,380 --> 00:37:30,697
are difficult to detect
using just the PLM.

857
00:37:32,033 --> 00:37:33,120
To reliably find these,

858
00:37:33,120 --> 00:37:36,093
you need transmission
electron microscopy or TEM.

859
00:37:37,080 --> 00:37:38,940
- [Sean] Where we top out
of about a thousand times

860
00:37:38,940 --> 00:37:40,710
with electro, with light microscopy,

861
00:37:40,710 --> 00:37:44,370
this tops out at about a million times.

862
00:37:44,370 --> 00:37:48,900
But what we need to see is
just what are the finest fibers

863
00:37:48,900 --> 00:37:51,330
that potentially can go into your lung.

864
00:37:51,330 --> 00:37:53,280
- [Gregor] Without having used a TEM,

865
00:37:53,280 --> 00:37:56,220
the EPA declared New York's air safe.

866
00:37:56,220 --> 00:37:57,870
- Everything we've tested for,

867
00:37:57,870 --> 00:37:59,790
which includes asbestos, lead,

868
00:37:59,790 --> 00:38:03,120
and VOCs have been below
any level of concern

869
00:38:03,120 --> 00:38:04,620
for the general public health.

870
00:38:06,240 --> 00:38:08,280
- [Gregor] But some researchers after 911

871
00:38:08,280 --> 00:38:10,890
actually did do studies with TEM.

872
00:38:10,890 --> 00:38:12,330
They found asbestos levels

873
00:38:12,330 --> 00:38:14,850
far above the EPA's own safety thresholds

874
00:38:14,850 --> 00:38:16,440
in most of their samples.

875
00:38:16,440 --> 00:38:18,240
And the report also warned that

876
00:38:18,240 --> 00:38:19,680
because many of these fibers

877
00:38:19,680 --> 00:38:21,540
were actually smaller than normal,

878
00:38:21,540 --> 00:38:23,910
they were especially dangerous.

879
00:38:23,910 --> 00:38:25,080
They posted the results

880
00:38:25,080 --> 00:38:28,110
on the American Industrial
Hygiene Association website,

881
00:38:28,110 --> 00:38:31,470
but within hours, their post disappeared.

882
00:38:31,470 --> 00:38:33,300
Less than 24 hours later,

883
00:38:33,300 --> 00:38:36,240
the researchers were notified
they been taken off the job

884
00:38:36,240 --> 00:38:39,210
and were no longer
required at Ground Zero.

885
00:38:39,210 --> 00:38:42,600
One former EPA chief
investigator later went on CBS

886
00:38:42,600 --> 00:38:46,020
saying they believe the
agency had deliberately used

887
00:38:46,020 --> 00:38:49,143
the wrong testing methods
and downplayed the danger.

888
00:38:50,040 --> 00:38:54,390
- New York City directly
lied about the test results

889
00:38:54,390 --> 00:38:55,890
for asbestos in air.

890
00:38:55,890 --> 00:38:59,043
When they finally released
them, they doctored the result.

891
00:39:00,360 --> 00:39:02,550
- We don't know if that's
true, but to be clear,

892
00:39:02,550 --> 00:39:05,130
PLM is still widely
used to detect asbestos

893
00:39:05,130 --> 00:39:08,880
because it's faster, it's
cheaper, it's easier to deploy.

894
00:39:08,880 --> 00:39:10,830
But what we do know is two things.

895
00:39:10,830 --> 00:39:13,680
First, the PLM method
was not sensitive enough

896
00:39:13,680 --> 00:39:15,720
to detect whether there
were asbestos fibers

897
00:39:15,720 --> 00:39:17,370
in the dust at Ground Zero.

898
00:39:17,370 --> 00:39:20,040
And second, the EPA did have other,

899
00:39:20,040 --> 00:39:22,770
more sensitive methods available to them.

900
00:39:22,770 --> 00:39:25,140
Whatever the motives,
the result was the same.

901
00:39:25,140 --> 00:39:26,340
New Yorkers were told

902
00:39:26,340 --> 00:39:29,010
that the air was safe
when it really wasn't.

903
00:39:29,010 --> 00:39:31,213
And as of December, 2023,

904
00:39:31,213 --> 00:39:34,770
6,781 of those who have been registered

905
00:39:34,770 --> 00:39:37,770
with the World Trade Center
Health Program have died

906
00:39:37,770 --> 00:39:40,170
either of an illness or a cancer

907
00:39:40,170 --> 00:39:43,208
linked just to their time
being around Ground Zero.

908
00:39:43,208 --> 00:39:47,880
(suspenseful music)
(sirens wailing)

909
00:39:47,880 --> 00:39:50,070
But even if the EPA had used the TEM,

910
00:39:50,070 --> 00:39:52,050
the answer would still not be simple

911
00:39:52,050 --> 00:39:56,040
because even then researchers
run into a more basic problem.

912
00:39:56,040 --> 00:39:59,190
What actually counts as asbestos?

913
00:39:59,190 --> 00:40:00,510
- Is there asbestos in the air?

914
00:40:00,510 --> 00:40:01,890
Is there asbestos in the soil?

915
00:40:01,890 --> 00:40:03,210
Is there asbestos in the water?

916
00:40:03,210 --> 00:40:05,010
Is there asbestos in the body?

917
00:40:05,010 --> 00:40:07,980
All of those counting
rules are based on fibers

918
00:40:07,980 --> 00:40:10,110
that are not super-long,

919
00:40:10,110 --> 00:40:14,580
but they're way longer than
the vast majority of say,

920
00:40:14,580 --> 00:40:16,380
Libby amphibole fibers

921
00:40:16,380 --> 00:40:20,460
and the vast majority of
fibers that are inhaled.

922
00:40:20,460 --> 00:40:23,100
So they're not even counting those,

923
00:40:23,100 --> 00:40:24,903
they're not even looking for them.

924
00:40:25,770 --> 00:40:28,290
The ways that we are right now

925
00:40:28,290 --> 00:40:30,960
telling people whether
they're being exposed or not

926
00:40:30,960 --> 00:40:32,460
is a lie!

927
00:40:32,460 --> 00:40:34,350
- [Gregor] And when longer fibers break

928
00:40:34,350 --> 00:40:36,731
forming these so-called
cleavage fragments,

929
00:40:36,731 --> 00:40:37,564
they don't count either.

930
00:40:37,564 --> 00:40:39,637
- Yeah, there's a whole effort to say,

931
00:40:39,637 --> 00:40:43,440
"Oh yeah, if it's been
broken, it's not dangerous."

932
00:40:43,440 --> 00:40:45,390
But there are so many
papers out there that show

933
00:40:45,390 --> 00:40:48,030
that if you put pure
cleavage fragments into mice,

934
00:40:48,030 --> 00:40:49,280
they get very, very sick.

935
00:40:50,580 --> 00:40:51,630
- [Gregor] This really matters

936
00:40:51,630 --> 00:40:53,070
when you're in a place like this

937
00:40:53,070 --> 00:40:54,150
and you realize the dust

938
00:40:54,150 --> 00:40:56,430
could be considered asbestos-contaminated

939
00:40:56,430 --> 00:40:57,780
under one definition,

940
00:40:57,780 --> 00:41:00,120
and perfectly safe under another.

941
00:41:00,120 --> 00:41:03,720
- [Researcher] Nobody would've
expected to find asbestos here.

942
00:41:03,720 --> 00:41:04,770
- [Gregor] To be clear,

943
00:41:04,770 --> 00:41:07,530
there were no asbestos mines in Nevada,

944
00:41:07,530 --> 00:41:08,820
no industrial sites,

945
00:41:08,820 --> 00:41:11,730
no history of asbestos
commercial use at all.

946
00:41:11,730 --> 00:41:13,560
But geologists, Brenda Buck

947
00:41:13,560 --> 00:41:17,070
and Rod Metcalf found asbestos
spread across approximately

948
00:41:17,070 --> 00:41:19,773
1 million acres outside Las Vegas.

949
00:41:21,570 --> 00:41:24,780
- [Rod] Geologic processes
transport these materials.

950
00:41:24,780 --> 00:41:27,420
And you know, before the erosion started,

951
00:41:27,420 --> 00:41:30,870
they were just in the bedrocks
along the mountain front.

952
00:41:30,870 --> 00:41:34,890
Now they're in sediments down
they're, in the stream here.

953
00:41:34,890 --> 00:41:35,723
- [Brenda] And the problem

954
00:41:35,723 --> 00:41:37,860
with the naturally
occurring stuff like this is

955
00:41:37,860 --> 00:41:40,950
it may be only a small
percentage in the rock

956
00:41:40,950 --> 00:41:43,560
and even a smaller percentage in the soil,

957
00:41:43,560 --> 00:41:45,510
but this stuff gets in the air.

958
00:41:45,510 --> 00:41:47,910
- [Gregor] Entire communities
might be breathing it in

959
00:41:47,910 --> 00:41:50,040
and getting sick without knowing.

960
00:41:50,040 --> 00:41:52,770
So Brenda and Rod tried to warn people.

961
00:41:52,770 --> 00:41:55,770
Back in late 2012, they
compiled all of their findings

962
00:41:55,770 --> 00:41:56,670
for a presentation

963
00:41:56,670 --> 00:41:59,790
at the Conference of the
Geological Society of America.

964
00:41:59,790 --> 00:42:01,590
But before the conference even began,

965
00:42:01,590 --> 00:42:04,350
the abstract caught the
attention of a journalist

966
00:42:04,350 --> 00:42:06,360
who reported on the story,

967
00:42:06,360 --> 00:42:09,060
and that's when the pushback started.

968
00:42:09,060 --> 00:42:12,510
Soon the state of Nevada sent
a cease and desist letter,

969
00:42:12,510 --> 00:42:15,870
and officials questioned
Brenda and Rod's methods.

970
00:42:15,870 --> 00:42:18,240
- So if you go to Las Vegas,
you're gonna get exposed

971
00:42:18,240 --> 00:42:20,340
to asbestos that they
didn't want that out there.

972
00:42:20,340 --> 00:42:22,920
- Every time I drove into Boulder City,

973
00:42:22,920 --> 00:42:26,607
there was an official
tailing me within a minute.

974
00:42:26,607 --> 00:42:30,480
- The message was clear,
don't look any further.

975
00:42:30,480 --> 00:42:32,490
Well, we did decide to look further.

976
00:42:32,490 --> 00:42:34,500
So we drove out into the desert

977
00:42:34,500 --> 00:42:36,690
to a popular off-roading spot

978
00:42:36,690 --> 00:42:39,077
to test whether there really is asbestos

979
00:42:39,077 --> 00:42:41,730
in the dust around Las Vegas.

980
00:42:41,730 --> 00:42:43,620
Okay, I'm strapped into a dune buggy here.

981
00:42:43,620 --> 00:42:45,076
I'm going to go down in that basin,

982
00:42:45,076 --> 00:42:48,270
and Sean's strapped up
some dust collectors

983
00:42:48,270 --> 00:42:50,550
with receivers in my breathing zone

984
00:42:50,550 --> 00:42:51,630
so we can actually figure out

985
00:42:51,630 --> 00:42:53,430
how much asbestos I would be inhaling

986
00:42:53,430 --> 00:42:55,604
through the dust that I kick up.

987
00:42:55,604 --> 00:42:59,342
(dune buggy engine spluttering)

988
00:42:59,342 --> 00:43:04,342
(tires roaring)
(soft rock music)

989
00:43:05,817 --> 00:43:07,666
(Gregor laughing)

990
00:43:07,666 --> 00:43:08,499
Woo!

991
00:43:14,034 --> 00:43:14,890
You see those donuts, huh?

992
00:43:14,890 --> 00:43:17,040
- [Sean] Yeah, yeah, you were
ripping it up down there.

993
00:43:17,040 --> 00:43:19,320
- [Gregor] Yeah, from a
geological point of view,

994
00:43:19,320 --> 00:43:20,400
any notes?

995
00:43:20,400 --> 00:43:21,300
- It rained today.

996
00:43:21,300 --> 00:43:22,650
I thought about that while I was watching,

997
00:43:22,650 --> 00:43:24,390
I was like, "There
might not be much dust,"

998
00:43:24,390 --> 00:43:26,070
but these are the air samples

999
00:43:26,070 --> 00:43:27,330
that were hanging in your
breathing room, right?

1000
00:43:27,330 --> 00:43:28,163
- Yes, yeah, yeah.

1001
00:43:28,163 --> 00:43:28,996
- And I looked at them

1002
00:43:28,996 --> 00:43:30,480
and the filters have some tanning on them.

1003
00:43:30,480 --> 00:43:31,530
- Oh, that's good.

1004
00:43:31,530 --> 00:43:33,840
- Which means we actually
did get some dust.

1005
00:43:33,840 --> 00:43:35,670
We don't know what's in it yet,

1006
00:43:35,670 --> 00:43:37,530
but there's something in there.

1007
00:43:37,530 --> 00:43:38,760
- But the original plan was for us

1008
00:43:38,760 --> 00:43:40,920
to actually do this at the dry lake bed

1009
00:43:40,920 --> 00:43:42,270
just outside Boulder City,

1010
00:43:42,270 --> 00:43:44,370
because this is where
people do the majority

1011
00:43:44,370 --> 00:43:45,360
of their off-roading,

1012
00:43:45,360 --> 00:43:46,950
you know, they camp, they do photo shoots,

1013
00:43:46,950 --> 00:43:49,170
even take their wedding photos,

1014
00:43:49,170 --> 00:43:51,000
except not on the day that we were there.

1015
00:43:51,000 --> 00:43:55,410
Okay, we're out here on the
dry lake bed, supposedly

1016
00:43:55,410 --> 00:43:59,310
in Las Vegas, where on the
one day that we're here,

1017
00:43:59,310 --> 00:44:01,320
the lake has decided not to be dry

1018
00:44:01,320 --> 00:44:03,240
in any sort of definition.

1019
00:44:03,240 --> 00:44:04,073
Oh my God.

1020
00:44:04,073 --> 00:44:06,720
And so what we're gonna go do is suit up,

1021
00:44:06,720 --> 00:44:07,650
get some samples,

1022
00:44:07,650 --> 00:44:09,420
and figure out how much asbestos

1023
00:44:09,420 --> 00:44:12,162
there really is in this stuff.

1024
00:44:12,162 --> 00:44:17,162
(suspenseful music)
(people softly chattering)

1025
00:44:17,706 --> 00:44:18,873
- [Sean] Okay.

1026
00:44:23,160 --> 00:44:24,120
We're gonna do a third.

1027
00:44:24,120 --> 00:44:26,325
Let's go out to that island.

1028
00:44:26,325 --> 00:44:28,980
(suspenseful music continues)

1029
00:44:28,980 --> 00:44:30,480
Okay.

1030
00:44:30,480 --> 00:44:32,610
- Okay, got the samples.

1031
00:44:32,610 --> 00:44:35,133
Next step, take 'em to the lab.

1032
00:44:35,980 --> 00:44:38,038
(car door thunks)

1033
00:44:38,038 --> 00:44:39,840
(suspenseful music)

1034
00:44:39,840 --> 00:44:42,106
- Great to see you guys again.

1035
00:44:42,106 --> 00:44:42,939
- Yeah, good to see you too.

1036
00:44:42,939 --> 00:44:45,780
Well, we are here for one thing I suppose,

1037
00:44:45,780 --> 00:44:48,360
like what kind of results did we get?

1038
00:44:48,360 --> 00:44:51,693
- Now the big reveal, drum roll please.

1039
00:44:53,130 --> 00:44:55,890
I did the dune buggy air samples first.

1040
00:44:55,890 --> 00:44:57,510
Those samples that you had on your left

1041
00:44:57,510 --> 00:44:58,800
and right shoulders,

1042
00:44:58,800 --> 00:45:01,260
I didn't find any asbestos fibers.

1043
00:45:01,260 --> 00:45:02,940
- Okay, well, I guess
that's a bit of a relief.

1044
00:45:02,940 --> 00:45:04,590
I'm glad we did the demonstration

1045
00:45:04,590 --> 00:45:06,570
and I'm kind of glad
we didn't find anything

1046
00:45:06,570 --> 00:45:09,090
because I'm pretty sure I took
my mask off at a few points.

1047
00:45:09,090 --> 00:45:12,240
- I've been in those shoes,
like, we didn't find anything.

1048
00:45:12,240 --> 00:45:13,950
Oh, but wait a minute,

1049
00:45:13,950 --> 00:45:17,040
I was breathing that (beep). (laughing)

1050
00:45:17,040 --> 00:45:18,750
- Exactly. (chuckling)

1051
00:45:18,750 --> 00:45:21,420
So what about the samples
in the dry lake bed?

1052
00:45:21,420 --> 00:45:24,693
- Ah, another drum roll.

1053
00:45:25,680 --> 00:45:28,770
- Okay.
- I found amphibole asbestos.

1054
00:45:28,770 --> 00:45:30,180
- Wow, okay.

1055
00:45:30,180 --> 00:45:32,760
- It's there. It is there.

1056
00:45:32,760 --> 00:45:35,220
I counted up a number of fibers,

1057
00:45:35,220 --> 00:45:37,560
the area of the filter they analyzed,

1058
00:45:37,560 --> 00:45:41,220
and I figured out that we had between 30

1059
00:45:41,220 --> 00:45:46,220
and 50 million asbestos
structures per gram of mud

1060
00:45:50,430 --> 00:45:51,480
that we were walking through.

1061
00:45:51,480 --> 00:45:52,313
- Whoa.

1062
00:45:53,333 --> 00:45:56,430
Just to think that we pulled
off to the side of the road,

1063
00:45:56,430 --> 00:45:59,190
walked what, 30 meters,
took three samples,

1064
00:45:59,190 --> 00:46:02,130
and all of them had these
incredibly high concentrations

1065
00:46:02,130 --> 00:46:03,225
of asbestos right there,

1066
00:46:03,225 --> 00:46:04,357
it's not like we had to go out and find.

1067
00:46:04,357 --> 00:46:05,550
- Right.

1068
00:46:05,550 --> 00:46:09,960
Very wet soil was lucky for us

1069
00:46:09,960 --> 00:46:13,290
because we know there's
asbestos in that soil.

1070
00:46:13,290 --> 00:46:14,700
Now think about the guys

1071
00:46:14,700 --> 00:46:16,590
that go taking their jeep across there

1072
00:46:16,590 --> 00:46:18,690
when it is a dry lake.

1073
00:46:18,690 --> 00:46:20,310
- I mean, that was our initial plan

1074
00:46:20,310 --> 00:46:22,407
to do the dune bugging there.

1075
00:46:22,407 --> 00:46:23,640
And I can't like help

1076
00:46:23,640 --> 00:46:26,070
but think about people drive
down that road all the time.

1077
00:46:26,070 --> 00:46:28,140
They must pull over,
they must go down there,

1078
00:46:28,140 --> 00:46:30,480
like just kick dust and rocks and-

1079
00:46:30,480 --> 00:46:32,970
- [Sean] We know they do. We know they do.

1080
00:46:32,970 --> 00:46:35,040
- And there's no sign to tell you

1081
00:46:35,040 --> 00:46:37,340
that there's anything
wrong with the dry lake.

1082
00:46:39,660 --> 00:46:41,733
It's not like we, you
know, discovered this,

1083
00:46:41,733 --> 00:46:44,253
this has been available data since-

1084
00:46:45,300 --> 00:46:48,930
- [Sean] October of 2013 is
the actual publication date

1085
00:46:48,930 --> 00:46:50,820
of naturally occurring asbestos,

1086
00:46:50,820 --> 00:46:52,260
potential for human exposure

1087
00:46:52,260 --> 00:46:55,683
in Southern Nevada by Brenda Buck et al.

1088
00:46:56,940 --> 00:46:58,770
- So 13 years we've had this data.

1089
00:46:58,770 --> 00:47:00,870
It's not like we'd rediscovered anything.

1090
00:47:00,870 --> 00:47:02,250
- And that's the other
thing that's really hard

1091
00:47:02,250 --> 00:47:03,630
about this science.

1092
00:47:03,630 --> 00:47:05,520
You need the public to be aware,

1093
00:47:05,520 --> 00:47:07,440
but you don't want to terrify them.

1094
00:47:07,440 --> 00:47:09,423
And so how do you find the right way?

1095
00:47:11,190 --> 00:47:12,390
- [Gregor] This will potentially be seen

1096
00:47:12,390 --> 00:47:13,740
by tens of millions of people.

1097
00:47:13,740 --> 00:47:16,350
So is there a message you want to get out?

1098
00:47:16,350 --> 00:47:19,740
- This is a natural hazard,

1099
00:47:19,740 --> 00:47:21,870
just like a lot of things in your life,

1100
00:47:21,870 --> 00:47:24,150
it's really good for
people to know whether

1101
00:47:24,150 --> 00:47:26,610
or not their house is in
a flooding zone, right?

1102
00:47:26,610 --> 00:47:29,010
It's really good to know
about earthquake risks.

1103
00:47:29,010 --> 00:47:32,340
It's really good to know about
hurricanes and tornadoes.

1104
00:47:32,340 --> 00:47:35,370
Well, this is just another natural hazard,

1105
00:47:35,370 --> 00:47:37,320
and if you have the information,

1106
00:47:37,320 --> 00:47:40,800
then you can make better decisions
to live a healthier life.

1107
00:47:41,668 --> 00:47:44,251
(gentle music)

1108
00:47:55,500 --> 00:47:56,333
- [Gregor] One big problem

1109
00:47:56,333 --> 00:47:58,110
is that asbestos in the real world

1110
00:47:58,110 --> 00:48:00,480
doesn't line up with how it's regulated.

1111
00:48:00,480 --> 00:48:01,710
Take this fiber,

1112
00:48:01,710 --> 00:48:04,470
it comes from a sample
of the same blue asbestos

1113
00:48:04,470 --> 00:48:06,240
from the site outside Las Vegas

1114
00:48:06,240 --> 00:48:08,430
we visited at the start of the video.

1115
00:48:08,430 --> 00:48:11,160
- [Sean] I think we have
two different phases,

1116
00:48:11,160 --> 00:48:14,190
at least of amphibole here

1117
00:48:14,190 --> 00:48:16,920
because if it's yellow
in this orientation,

1118
00:48:16,920 --> 00:48:20,280
but also blue in this orientation
in the same fiber bundle,

1119
00:48:20,280 --> 00:48:25,113
then we have a change in
the phase from here to here.

1120
00:48:26,100 --> 00:48:29,910
- [Gregor] So this one fiber
is actually two minerals.

1121
00:48:29,910 --> 00:48:32,880
And that complexity shows
up in its structure too,

1122
00:48:32,880 --> 00:48:34,560
under the electron microscope,

1123
00:48:34,560 --> 00:48:37,170
one side could meet the
definition of asbestos,

1124
00:48:37,170 --> 00:48:39,780
the other may not simply
because of its shape.

1125
00:48:39,780 --> 00:48:43,860
So one single fiber could fall
within one of the six named

1126
00:48:43,860 --> 00:48:47,130
and regulated asbestos minerals
if you look at one side,

1127
00:48:47,130 --> 00:48:50,910
but the other would be
completely unregulated.

1128
00:48:50,910 --> 00:48:53,560
Your lungs don't care about
these categories, though.

1129
00:48:54,450 --> 00:48:57,690
- [Narrator] The asbestos fiber,
you'll find it everywhere.

1130
00:48:57,690 --> 00:49:01,560
No ordinary rock, no single rock indeed,

1131
00:49:01,560 --> 00:49:03,180
but a group of related minerals

1132
00:49:03,180 --> 00:49:06,570
with characteristics in
common, but in varying degrees,

1133
00:49:06,570 --> 00:49:07,987
- Most experts will say,

1134
00:49:07,987 --> 00:49:11,460
"Asbestos isn't a mineral
or a geologic term,

1135
00:49:11,460 --> 00:49:16,260
it's a commercial one,"
but that's symbolics.

1136
00:49:16,260 --> 00:49:21,260
Why do I say that? Because it's
not just a commercial term.

1137
00:49:21,390 --> 00:49:23,850
We now know that asbestos can kill you.

1138
00:49:23,850 --> 00:49:25,080
So if we're gonna say that,

1139
00:49:25,080 --> 00:49:28,980
we have to define it based on
health effect, and we don't.

1140
00:49:28,980 --> 00:49:30,960
- [Gregor] So what are we
actually doing about it?

1141
00:49:30,960 --> 00:49:33,300
The system was so complex,
it was so burdensome

1142
00:49:33,300 --> 00:49:37,560
that our country hasn't even
been able to uphold a ban

1143
00:49:37,560 --> 00:49:41,730
on asbestos, a known
carcinogen that kills as many

1144
00:49:41,730 --> 00:49:44,400
as 10,000 Americans every year.

1145
00:49:44,400 --> 00:49:45,360
- [Gregor] Well, in 2016,

1146
00:49:45,360 --> 00:49:47,880
Congress did try to
fix this broken system.

1147
00:49:47,880 --> 00:49:50,460
They passed an amendment
giving the EPA new power

1148
00:49:50,460 --> 00:49:53,040
to evaluate and restrict
dangerous chemicals,

1149
00:49:53,040 --> 00:49:54,453
including asbestos.

1150
00:49:55,470 --> 00:49:58,590
- I think it's time to
sign the Frank R. Ladenburg

1151
00:49:58,590 --> 00:50:02,070
Chemical Safety for the
21st Century Act into law.

1152
00:50:02,070 --> 00:50:04,983
- [Gregor] But then
progress stalled, again.

1153
00:50:06,360 --> 00:50:07,950
Under the Trump administration,

1154
00:50:07,950 --> 00:50:11,910
efforts to strengthen asbestos
rules slowed dramatically.

1155
00:50:11,910 --> 00:50:14,050
Trump had publicly praised
asbestos for years.

1156
00:50:14,050 --> 00:50:16,350
- A lot of people in my
industry think asbestos

1157
00:50:16,350 --> 00:50:20,310
is the greatest fireproofing
material ever, ever made!

1158
00:50:20,310 --> 00:50:22,410
- It wasn't until 2024

1159
00:50:22,410 --> 00:50:25,560
that the US finally banned
chrysotile asbestos.

1160
00:50:25,560 --> 00:50:27,180
But this ban doesn't cover

1161
00:50:27,180 --> 00:50:29,100
the other five types of asbestos,

1162
00:50:29,100 --> 00:50:32,070
and it still allows some
manufacturers up to 12 years

1163
00:50:32,070 --> 00:50:33,870
to phase it out.

1164
00:50:33,870 --> 00:50:35,670
It doesn't address what
to do with asbestos

1165
00:50:35,670 --> 00:50:36,780
already in schools

1166
00:50:36,780 --> 00:50:38,790
and homes and other buildings,

1167
00:50:38,790 --> 00:50:40,560
nor does it fix any of the numerous

1168
00:50:40,560 --> 00:50:42,270
classification, identification,

1169
00:50:42,270 --> 00:50:44,040
and detection loopholes,

1170
00:50:44,040 --> 00:50:46,953
and it doesn't address the
asbestos in the environment.

1171
00:50:47,790 --> 00:50:50,913
On top of that, the EPA is
already getting sued, again.

1172
00:50:52,470 --> 00:50:55,650
- There have been tremendous forces

1173
00:50:55,650 --> 00:50:58,050
from commercial industries

1174
00:50:58,050 --> 00:51:01,620
to make it sound like
it's not as bad as it is,

1175
00:51:01,620 --> 00:51:05,460
and to find ways to allow them

1176
00:51:05,460 --> 00:51:08,291
to continue to use the material.

1177
00:51:08,291 --> 00:51:12,780
This is a sad, sad fact
of our decision-making

1178
00:51:12,780 --> 00:51:13,613
in our country

1179
00:51:13,613 --> 00:51:16,170
and other countries, is
that it's driven by money.

1180
00:51:16,170 --> 00:51:17,940
- But at least the United States are going

1181
00:51:17,940 --> 00:51:19,500
for some level of moderation.

1182
00:51:19,500 --> 00:51:21,420
Other countries are not that lucky.

1183
00:51:21,420 --> 00:51:26,420
In 2019, India imported more
than 350,000 tons of asbestos,

1184
00:51:26,580 --> 00:51:29,310
and it's predicted that
in the upcoming decades,

1185
00:51:29,310 --> 00:51:30,480
6 million people there

1186
00:51:30,480 --> 00:51:32,850
might develop asbestos-related diseases.

1187
00:51:32,850 --> 00:51:34,320
And similar things are happening

1188
00:51:34,320 --> 00:51:36,480
in many of the other countries in Asia.

1189
00:51:36,480 --> 00:51:38,280
We've actually found this website

1190
00:51:38,280 --> 00:51:41,400
where it looks like you
can just buy asbestos cloth

1191
00:51:41,400 --> 00:51:44,760
made in China, but please don't.

1192
00:51:44,760 --> 00:51:47,280
And all of the asbestos
that we've already mined,

1193
00:51:47,280 --> 00:51:50,700
even after we stop using
it, it's still out there.

1194
00:51:50,700 --> 00:51:54,330
Asbestos doesn't naturally
decay in the environment.

1195
00:51:54,330 --> 00:51:56,460
So should you be worried?

1196
00:51:56,460 --> 00:51:59,130
Well, having asbestos in your house

1197
00:51:59,130 --> 00:52:01,500
doesn't automatically
mean that it's dangerous.

1198
00:52:01,500 --> 00:52:03,330
If you have asbestos in your ceiling

1199
00:52:03,330 --> 00:52:06,240
and you don't drill into it,
you're probably gonna be fine.

1200
00:52:06,240 --> 00:52:09,573
Asbestos is an issue if
the particles go airborne,

1201
00:52:10,530 --> 00:52:13,890
but who knows which house has asbestos,

1202
00:52:13,890 --> 00:52:15,630
where all of that asbestos is,

1203
00:52:15,630 --> 00:52:17,550
who's gonna take care of it and how?

1204
00:52:17,550 --> 00:52:19,830
So a lot of the answers to these questions

1205
00:52:19,830 --> 00:52:21,690
just don't exist yet.

1206
00:52:21,690 --> 00:52:24,750
But if you're worried about
asbestos exposure for yourself,

1207
00:52:24,750 --> 00:52:27,353
check out the links that
we've put in the description.

1208
00:52:28,980 --> 00:52:30,600
I think a big part of the problem is

1209
00:52:30,600 --> 00:52:33,540
that people assume
asbestos is a solved issue.

1210
00:52:33,540 --> 00:52:34,650
And I'll be the first to admit,

1211
00:52:34,650 --> 00:52:36,420
I fell for that line of thinking.

1212
00:52:36,420 --> 00:52:38,820
Here's the ending I
wrote for our PFAS video.

1213
00:52:38,820 --> 00:52:42,120
- We've been here before
with lead gasoline, Freon

1214
00:52:42,120 --> 00:52:45,210
and asbestos, and each
time we did the research

1215
00:52:45,210 --> 00:52:48,450
and made the right decision
to phase these chemicals out.

1216
00:52:48,450 --> 00:52:50,703
- Yeah, I was completely oblivious.

1217
00:52:52,830 --> 00:52:54,630
- We will look back at our history

1218
00:52:54,630 --> 00:52:57,030
and what do we do with tobacco?

1219
00:52:57,030 --> 00:52:58,980
Everybody was smoking, right?

1220
00:52:58,980 --> 00:53:00,570
It would be improper for me

1221
00:53:00,570 --> 00:53:02,100
to not offer an ashtray

1222
00:53:02,100 --> 00:53:04,530
even if I wasn't a smoker back in the day.

1223
00:53:04,530 --> 00:53:06,780
And all the scientists working

1224
00:53:06,780 --> 00:53:08,617
for the big cigarette companies said,

1225
00:53:08,617 --> 00:53:10,950
"Why, tobacco never hurt anybody."

1226
00:53:10,950 --> 00:53:14,100
But because of the outcry

1227
00:53:14,100 --> 00:53:18,840
and the recognition that
smoking causes disease,

1228
00:53:18,840 --> 00:53:20,460
everybody knows someone who died

1229
00:53:20,460 --> 00:53:21,910
because of cigarettes, right?

1230
00:53:23,580 --> 00:53:27,450
Now, you might find out that
asbestos-related diseases

1231
00:53:27,450 --> 00:53:30,690
has touched you in some way,
you don't even know yet.

1232
00:53:30,690 --> 00:53:34,740
I didn't know my grandfather
died because of asbestos,

1233
00:53:34,740 --> 00:53:38,280
that my father is dying
more likely than not

1234
00:53:38,280 --> 00:53:39,420
because of asbestos.

1235
00:53:39,420 --> 00:53:41,700
Did I know that when I started

1236
00:53:41,700 --> 00:53:44,220
looking at asbestos
under a microscope, no.

1237
00:53:44,220 --> 00:53:47,280
Did I know that when I changed
the brakes on the Jeeps

1238
00:53:47,280 --> 00:53:48,990
that I ran around in, no.

1239
00:53:48,990 --> 00:53:50,430
Did I know that when I ran around

1240
00:53:50,430 --> 00:53:53,910
through asbestos-containing dust, no.

1241
00:53:53,910 --> 00:53:54,743
Now I do.

1242
00:53:56,130 --> 00:53:57,510
- Pretty much every scientist

1243
00:53:57,510 --> 00:53:59,310
and journalist we spoke to for this video

1244
00:53:59,310 --> 00:54:00,540
said the same thing.

1245
00:54:00,540 --> 00:54:02,760
This is a hard story to get out there.

1246
00:54:02,760 --> 00:54:05,670
They've faced economic
pressure, political pressure,

1247
00:54:05,670 --> 00:54:07,140
the research got buried,

1248
00:54:07,140 --> 00:54:09,270
and some people even
received death threats

1249
00:54:09,270 --> 00:54:10,920
for reporting on the story.

1250
00:54:10,920 --> 00:54:13,350
It is an uncomfortable topic,

1251
00:54:13,350 --> 00:54:15,480
but I think it's these
uncomfortable topics

1252
00:54:15,480 --> 00:54:16,380
that matter the most,

1253
00:54:16,380 --> 00:54:18,660
that have the potential
to do the most good,

1254
00:54:18,660 --> 00:54:20,280
yet they are also the ones

1255
00:54:20,280 --> 00:54:22,650
that are the most uncomfortable to watch.

1256
00:54:22,650 --> 00:54:25,530
So I really appreciate you
for sticking around to the end

1257
00:54:25,530 --> 00:54:28,200
and facing the truth,
making yourself aware

1258
00:54:28,200 --> 00:54:30,390
and becoming part of the solution.

1259
00:54:30,390 --> 00:54:34,332
So perhaps now more than
ever, thank you for watching.

1260
00:54:34,332 --> 00:54:39,332
(screen chiming)
(suspenseful music)

